
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'Matthew, Mark and Luke each record two short comparisons after Jesus has spoken of the bridegroom. No man puts a piece of new cloth on an old garment, because the piece takes from the garment and the rent is made worse. Men do not put new wine into old bottles, because the bottles break and the wine runs out, but new wine is put into new bottles, and in Matthew and Luke both are preserved. Luke adds that a man who has drunk old wine does not straightway desire new, because he says, “The old is better.” Jesus gives no explanation of any part of the stories and names none of them. The comparisons say that joining new to old brings loss, that what fits is kept, and that a man used to the old does not straightway want the new. Jesus spoke during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap, and the Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that says what the cloth, the garment, the wine or the bottles are, or that makes following, feasting or putting in a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('p', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** a piece of new cloth put on an old garment makes the tear worse; new wine put into old bottles breaks them and spills the wine; new wine in new bottles is kept, and Luke adds the saying about old wine.',
    '**The meaning:** joining new to old brings loss; what fits is preserved; a man used to the old does not straightway desire the new.',
    '**The boundaries:** no explanation from Jesus; no identity for the cloth, the garment, the wine, the bottles or the bridegroom; the saying about the bridegroom is not the explanation of the comparisons.',
    '**The present lesson:** fitness, foresight, care for what is valuable and honesty about habit, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9
A(('h1', S9))
A(('bul', [
    '**Between accounts.** Matthew has “new cloth” and “both are preserved.” Mark has “seweth” and “new wine must be put into new bottles,” with no “both are preserved.” Luke has “a new garment,” calls the saying a parable, and alone adds the old wine.',
    '**Between actions.** A piece is put on a garment. Wine is put into bottles. The first ends in a worse tear and the second in broken bottles and spilled wine, or in both preserved.',
    '**Between settings.** The question about fasting comes from John’s disciples in Matthew, from John’s disciples and the Pharisees’ in Mark, and from “they” in Luke.',
    '**Between these comparisons and the saying about the bridegroom.** Jesus’ words about the bridegroom answer the question about fasting. The cloth and the wine are not given as their explanation.',
    '**What must not be assigned.** No identity for the cloth, the garment, the wine, the bottles, the man or the bridegroom. The Body of Christ is none of them.',
    '**What must not be imported.** No meaning carried in from the generation passage, the Lost Sheep, the Wise and Foolish Builders or the Sabbath scene.',
    '**What must not be made of the actions.** Following, feasting and putting in are actions in a story. They are not steps by which a person is saved.',
]))

