A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Barren Fig Tree and the Generation’s Response, the Lost Sheep and the Lost Coin, and the Wise and Foolish Builders are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. These comparisons make no use of their meanings, and they make none of these.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. After Jesus speaks of the bridegroom, He says that no man puts a piece of new cloth on an old garment, because the tear is made worse, and that new wine is put into new bottles, because new wine in old bottles breaks them and spills the wine. In Luke a man who has drunk old wine says, “The old is better.” Jesus gives no explanation and names none of the parts. The comparisons say that joining new to old brings loss and that what fits is kept.'))
